# --- build output (regenerated by dotnet build/publish) ---
GK/source/bin/
GK/source/obj/
GK/source/publish/
GK/rules/bin/
GK/rules/obj/
GK/smoke/bin/
GK/smoke/obj/
GK/playtest/bin/
GK/playtest/obj/
# Never commit the published exe. Dropping ReadyToRun and single-file compression in
# v1.11.0 (a 3.5x faster first launch — see the csproj) took it to ~155 MB, past
# GitHub's 100 MB per-file limit; an autosync run that swept it in made the push fail
# with an HTTP 408 that reads like a network problem and is not one. It ships the way
# GritKeeper/app/ already does: as an asset on the GitHub Release.
GK/publish/

# --- packaged deliverables (regenerated from GK/source; app/ is ~160 MB of
#     self-contained .NET runtime, the zip ~70 MB — both rebuilt on release) ---
GritKeeper/app/
GritKeeper.zip
# ...and the two book bundles that go on Releases the same way. Named by pattern after
# `git add -A` on 2026-08-10 happily staged BloodAndGrit-Modules.zip, which the rule above
# missed by one word. A release asset is not repo content.
BloodAndGrit-*.zip
# GritKeeper/source/ was a byte-identical second copy of GK/source tracked in git for no
# build reason — and the exact seam that silently diverged once (2026-07-10). It is now a
# generated mirror like app/: overwritten from GK/source as a step of packaging, never
# hand-edited, never carried between sessions in git. GK/source is the only source of truth.
GritKeeper/source/
# Same story for the rules library, mirrored beside source/ since v1.28.0 (the app's
# ProjectReference points at ..\rules\, so the two must ship as siblings).
GritKeeper/rules/
# And the license pair, copied in by package.ps1 from the repo root at v1.29.1 so the ZIP carries
# them. Copies, not sources — tracking them would put a second LICENSE in the repo that has to be
# kept in step with the first, which is the same duplicate-tracked-copy mistake GritKeeper/source/
# was. The root LICENSE and NOTICE are the only real ones.
GritKeeper/LICENSE
GritKeeper/NOTICE
# Per-release notes are a release asset like the zip: written to be pasted into the GitHub
# Release, and redundant the moment it is published — the text then lives on the Release and
# the history lives in CHANGELOG.md. Seven of them (v1.16.2–v1.20.1) accumulated at the repo
# root before this rule; they were removed on 2026-07-27, and are still recoverable from git
# history and from the Releases themselves. Write the next one, paste it, let it stay local.
# Widened from RELEASE_NOTES_v*.md on 2026-08-10: the modules notes are named
# RELEASE_NOTES_modules_v1.1.md and fell straight through it.
RELEASE_NOTES_*.md

# --- per-table runtime state (the Keeper's live session, not project source) ---
session.json
startup-error.txt

# --- local-only: what GitHub carries is the three books, GritKeeper, and what supports them ---
# Standing rule from 2026-07-29 (user's): the public repo holds the deliverables and the files
# that BUILD or CHECK them — nothing else. These six stay on this disk, still doing their jobs;
# they were untracked with `git rm --cached`, so they are recoverable from history if wanted.
#   * the machine's own setup — a Claude Code session command. It builds nothing and describes
#     this laptop, not the game. (The scheduled backup task that used to be listed here was
#     deleted on 2026-08-10 along with autosync.ps1 and register_autosync_task.ps1: nothing in
#     any repo runs on a timer now, and syncing happens in .githooks/post-merge when a branch
#     lands on main. The old files are recoverable from history if anyone wants to read them.)
#     Narrowed from a flat `.claude/` on 2026-08-22 to let one file through. `commands/ship.md`
#     is the ordered walk for cutting a release, and every tool it drives — package.ps1, sign.ps1,
#     make_bundles.py, release_index.py — is tracked here already. A runbook for building the
#     deliverables is a file that builds them, and it is worth nothing to a later session if it
#     lives only on this laptop. Four lines because that is the git idiom: a file cannot be
#     re-included out of an ignored directory, so `commands/` is un-ignored before ship.md is.
.claude/*
!.claude/commands/
.claude/commands/*
!.claude/commands/ship.md
#   * process records: an unbuilt design proposal and a log of editorial findings that were
#     declined. Both are conversation, not product. The online-play proposal's substance — the
#     four rungs and the two credential/RNG gotchas — is summarized in CLAUDE.md's roadmap so
#     nothing is lost by the file being local.
DESIGN-online-play.md
ONLINE-PLAY-BRIEF.md
editorial-denials.md
#   * the session handoffs and audit records. Same class: what was found and why a thing was done
#     the way it was, written for the next session rather than for a reader of the repo. Every one
#     of them has a durable half that IS published — the changes in CHANGELOG.md, the decisions in
#     GK/CLAUDE.md. They are listed here rather than merely left untracked because `git add -A`
#     sweeps an untracked file in without asking, and doing exactly that on 2026-08-16 staged
#     three of them plus a tree of build artifacts.
HANDOFF-*.md
AUDIT-*.md
PROGRAM-*.md
_combatlab/
#   * a dead one-shot: add_index.py baked the v2.9 Index into a source file that no longer exists
#     (player-src.html, retired 2026-07-18) and has been marked "do not re-run" ever since.
add_index.py
#   * a dev tool for a lab that is itself ignored: tools/mapdiff.mjs renders the SVGs under
#     _combatlab/_maps and reports which ones moved. Untracked 2026-08-27. Nothing in the repo
#     runs it, it defaults to a directory five lines above this one, and it needs three npm
#     packages installed globally that a fresh clone will not have, so a cloner who ran it would
#     get an import error rather than a map. It fails this file's own test twice over. Still on
#     disk, still works, recoverable from history.
tools/mapdiff.mjs

# --- python / editor debris ---
__pycache__/
*.pyc
_map_preview.html

# Runtime files package.ps1 moves out of GritKeeper\app before zipping. Never shipped,
# never committed — they are whatever the machine that ran the app happened to leave behind.
.package-aside/
_base/

# tools/extract_rules.py --out writes this: the six books as one JSON digest. Nothing in the repo
# reads the file — the audits import digest() and hold it in memory — so a committed copy would be
# a second, staler copy of books that are already tracked. Generated on demand, never versioned.
rules-digest.json
